--TEST--
`Core\Cap::has` reports the grant and never a scope, so a `true` still meets the door's own refusal for an argument outside it — ADR 0112 §§ 6-7, ADR 0118 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The member takes a capability and no argument to place inside it, so the
// question it can answer is "granted for something". Here that is `true`, and
// the grant behind it is one directory wide.
if (Core\Cap::has("fs.write")) {
    echo "fs.write: granted\n";
} else {
    echo "fs.write: not granted\n";
}

// The bound on the other side, and the reason ADR 0112 § 7 keeps two
// enforcement points rather than folding the second into the first: a path
// inside the grant opens, and one outside it is still refused at the door. A
// program that read the `true` above as permission for any path learns nothing
// new from it — `nvs_runtime::capability::require` asks again with the path in
// hand, which is the ask this member cannot make on its behalf.
Core\IO::write("inside.txt", "written\n");
echo Core\IO::read("inside.txt");

try {
    Core\IO::write("../outside.txt", "x");
    echo "../outside.txt: written\n";
} catch (IOError $reached) {
    echo "../outside.txt: the os answered first\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo "../outside.txt: refused at the door\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# One directory wide, which is what makes the two halves of the case differ: a
# grant that named nothing would refuse both, and `true` would be a lie.
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
fs.write: granted
written
../outside.txt: refused at the door
